    "https://careers.emich.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-special-education-ypsilanti-michigan-united-states-6bd072ec-b09e-4c53-9e4d-9ece88350c46": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Special Education)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor (Special Education) - Ypsilanti, Michigan, United States Assistant Professor (Special Education) - Ypsilanti, Michigan, United States Kiosk mode Assistant Professor (Special Education) Ypsilanti, Michigan, United States College of Education Academic & Student Affairs College of Education Faculty Full Time Opening on: Sep 1 2026 Closing on: Jan 10 2027 Title: Assistant Professor (Special Education) Employee Classification: FA - Faculty 16 and 24 Pay Pay Grade: FA 01 Division: Academic and Student Affairs Subdivision: Spec Ed and Comm Sci and Disorders Department: College of Education Campus Location: Main Campus General Summary The Department of Special Education & Communication Sciences and Disorders, College of Education at Eastern Michigan University, seeks applicants for a tenure-track faculty position within its Special Education Program. The appointment will be at the rank of Assistant Professor with a start in Fall 2027. Principal Duties and Responsibilities Principal Duties and Responsibilities : • Design and deliver in-person, hybrid, remote (e.g., satellite locations), and online instruction within two or more of the program’s current categorical disability areas (undergraduate and graduate levels) for which its special education administration specialist degree may be one of them, as well as within the program’s new forthcoming comprehensive undergraduate licensure pathway. • Design curriculum and enact pedagogy that reflect and support current trends of best practices, inclusive excellence, diversity, equity, state standards, and the special education programmatic needs of local K-12 school districts. • Provide academic and scholarly research advising to undergraduate and graduate students. • Engage in research and scholarly activities relative to the university’s R2 ranking and in accordance with the program’s criteria for tenure and promotion. • Provide service at the program, department, college, and university levels. • Meet all contractual obligations for tenure-track faculty. Minimum Qualifications: Required Qualifications: • Doctoral degree in Special Education or related field from an accredited institution, completed and conferred by date of appointment and no later than one week prior to the start of the 2027-2028 academic year. • Three or more years of P-12 special education teaching experiences related to the appointment. • Experience in and/or knowledge about the preparation of special education teacher candidates from traditional and non-traditional backgrounds, including first-generation, urban, and second-career/adult learners, as well as in-service educators pursuing advanced credentials • Experience in and/or knowledge about in-person, hybrid, remote, and/or online (asynchronous and/or synchronous) instructional design and delivery. • Experience in and/or knowledge about how to address diversity, equity, and inclusion within a special education teacher preparation program, including within a special education administration specialist degree (if applicable). • An emergent/active scholarly agenda related to the appointment and the program’s criteria for tenure and promotion. Preferred Qualifications: Desired Qualifications: • Previous higher education teaching experience related to the appointment • Experience with acquiring or pursuing external research funding Special Instructions: All applications must be made online at https://caereers.emich.edu/jobs. Applicants must submit the following materials for consideration: 1. A cover letter that addresses each of the above listed required and preferred qualifications. Particular attention should detail a teaching philosophy and approach to the design and delivery of instruction related to this position. Applicants without a conferred doctoral degree from an accredited institution at time of applying must also detail a list of doctoral/dissertation benchmarks and dates leading up to an anticipated date of graduation. 2. A detailed curriculum vitae; and 3. The names, titles, professional mailing addresses, email addresses, and telephone numbers of at least three references, but no more than five references Applicants without a conferred doctoral degree from an accredited institution at time of applying must include and identify their dissertation chair as one of the references. For more information contact Dr. John Palladino, search committee chair, 734.487.2769 or jpalladi@emich.edu or Dr. Jackie McGinnis, Department Head, 734-4873300 or jmcginnis@emich.edu Appointment Percentage: 100%% Apply Now! Want to hear about jobs like this one? Job Alert 823a90b1 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments College of Education College of Education Categories Academic & Student Affairs College of Education Academic & Student Affairs College of Education First Name (required) 0a6af474 Last Name (required) fe8e7bb8 Email (required) 5a3bc668 Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Similar Jobs Institutional Research Analyst, Senior (IRIM) Inst Research and Accreditation Develop and maintain systems that enhance the utility of the Institutional Research and Information Management (IRIM) data warehouse. Develop and maintain online and automated reporting facilities and interactive dashboards. Provide statistical support and enrollment forecasting. Collect and audit primary and secondary data sources to assure accuracy of data for analysis. Title IX Investigator Chief Diversity Officer Reporting to the University Title IX Coordinator, the Title IX Investigator will be the university's lead internal investigator in cases involving complaints of sexual misconduct, sexual harassment, and gender-related discrimination or violence, including stalking and intimate partner violence. The primary